"C6H11NO5", "iupac_name": "2-amino-4-hydroxy-4-methylpentanedioic acid", "common_name": "4-hydroxy-4-methylglutamic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H11NO5/c1-6(12,5(10)11)2-3(7)4(8)9/h3,12H,2,7H2,1H3,(H,8,9)(H,10,11)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Fe2+-catalyzed aldol condensation of pyruvate and glyoxylate", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343-353K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "ferrous iron (Fe2+)", "aqueous": true, "other": "anoxic vent-mixing-zone water; Fe2+-rich; near-neutral to mildly alkaline"}, "mechanism": "Fe2+ activation of pyruvate enables aldol addition to the aldehyde carbonyl of glyoxylate, forming the new C-C bond of the C5 product.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) directly demonstrated Fe2+-promoted conversion of pyruvate and glyoxylate to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate at about 343 K, and Springsteen et al. (2018) observed the same C3+C2 condensation within linked glyoxylate cycles. This is the best-supported carbon-skeleton-building entry into the glutamate network under iron-rich anoxic hydrothermal conditions."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Dehydration of 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_004", "mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343-353K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "ferrous iron (Fe2+)", "aqueous": true, "other": "anoxic vent-mixing-zone water; dehydration of the beta-hydroxy ketoacid"}, "mechanism": "Fe2+-assisted beta-elimination removes water from the beta-hydroxy ketoacid to give the conjugated unsaturated keto-diacid.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) identified dehydration of 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate to the conjugated enedione within the same iron-driven network. The step is chemically reasonable in warm aqueous Fe2+ solutions, although back-hydration and competing side reactions could lower natural yields."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Reduction of 4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid to 2-oxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343-353K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "ferrous iron (Fe2+)", "aqueous": true, "other": "anoxic, mildly reducing hydrothermal solution"}, "mechanism": "Reduction of the C=C bond in the unsaturated keto-diacid yields the saturated alpha-keto acid 2-oxoglutarate.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) reported reduction of the unsaturated C5 intermediate to 2-oxoglutarate under the same Fe2+-rich conditions. Lee et al. (2014) showed that 2-oxoglutarate is a recurrent hydrothermal product, but also noted broader keto-acid interconversion and decomposition chemistry, so retention of 2-oxoglutarate depends on kinetic context."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Fe0/Fe2+-promoted reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate with hydroxylamine", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["elemental iron (Fe0)", "ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "343-353K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "elemental iron (Fe0) and ferrous iron (Fe2+)", "aqueous": true, "other": "hydroxylamine present; Fe0 supplies electrons; racemic product expected"}, "mechanism": "Oxime or imine formation at the alpha-keto group is followed by iron-mediated reduction to furnish the alpha-amino acid.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) experimentally converted 2-oxoglutarate to glutamate by iron-promoted reductive amination with hydroxylamine. This is a strong mechanistic precedent, but the route is not uncritically preferred because prebiotic hydroxylamine abundance is debated; the product would also be racemic, so the network uses non-stereospecific glutamate."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "FeS-catalyzed reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate with ammonia", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["ferrous sulfide (FeS)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "ferrous sulfide (FeS)", "aqueous": true, "other": "alkaline vent, pH 9-11, H2-rich and anoxic; racemic product expected"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine with the alpha-keto group on FeS surfaces, and mineral-mediated reduction yields the amino acid.", "reasoning": "Kitadai et al. (2019) showed FeS catalyzes reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate with ammonia in alkaline hydrothermal settings. This route is geochemically attractive because FeS is common at vents and NH3 is a more plausible nitrogen donor than hydroxylamine, though direct selectivity comparisons against the Fe0/Fe2+ system remain open; the product is expected to be racemic."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Amination of 2-hydroxypentane-1,5-dinitrile", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "cyanosulfidic evaporitic pool; precursor pool from HCN-rich photochemistry; concentration during drying"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia replaces the alpha-hydroxyl functionality of the hydroxydinitrile, forming the aminodinitrile and water.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) reported amination of the C5 hydroxydinitrile to the corresponding aminodinitrile in cyanosulfidic chemistry. The transformation is experimentally grounded, but persistence of the C5 dinitrile pool under natural UV, pH cycling, and dilution has not been fully quantified."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrolysis of 2-aminopentane-1,5-dinitrile to glutamate", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "wet phases after concentration cycles; racemic product expected"}, "mechanism": "Sequential nitrile hydration to amides and then carboxylates converts both nitriles to the dicarboxylic amino acid, releasing ammonia.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) demonstrated hydrolysis of the aminodinitrile branch to glutamate, making this the key surface route that bypasses 2-oxoglutarate. Its plausibility is strong in concentrated wet phases, although field-scale yields would depend on keeping hydrolysis competitive with photolysis and dispersal; the product is expected to be racemic."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydrothermal oxidation of 2-hydroxyglutarate to 2-oxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "370-430K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "anoxic hydrothermal fluid with redox gradients; no discrete catalyst specified"}, "mechanism": "Alpha-hydroxy oxidation or dehydrogenation converts the C2 alcohol to the corresponding alpha-keto group.", "reasoning": "Lee et al. (2014) identified 2-hydroxyglutarate as a hydrothermal precursor or interconversion partner of 2-oxoglutarate. This makes alpha-hydroxy to alpha-keto oxidation a credible vent-process entry to the hub molecule, though the same hot-fluid chemistry can also drive further degradation."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Cyanosulfidic oxidation of 2-hydroxyglutarate to 2-oxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "UV-exposed cyanosulfidic waters with sulfur redox chemistry"}, "mechanism": "Cyanosulfidic redox chemistry oxidizes the C2 alcohol to the corresponding alpha-keto acid.", "reasoning": "Ritson (2021) incorporated 2-hydroxyglutarate oxidation to 2-oxoglutarate into a cyanosulfidic prebiotic Krebs-cycle analogue. This gives a surface route to the glutamate hub, but productive transfer of that hub into later amination chemistry remains a systems-level open question rather than a one-pot demonstration."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Decarboxylation of 3-oxalomalic acid to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_013"], "outputs": ["mol_003", "mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "296-300K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "ambient aqueous glyoxylate-cycle analogue; anoxic; conversion observed over hours"}, "mechanism": "Oxidative decarboxylation removes one carboxyl group from the C6 tricarboxylate, generating the C5 hydroxy-keto diacid.", "reasoning": "Springsteen et al. (2018) observed oxidative decarboxylation of 3-oxalomalic acid to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate at room temperature in aqueous solution. The reaction strongly supports a surface source of the downstream C5 intermediate, although coupling this ambient-temperature step into the hotter iron-driven sequence has not yet been shown continuously."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Hydrothermal decomposition of 2-amino-4-hydroxy-4-methylpentanedioic acid to 2-oxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_015"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_007"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "370-430K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "anoxic hydrothermal decomposition; no discrete catalyst specified"}, "mechanism": "Retro-aldol-like cleavage and oxidative deamination collapse the C6 amino-hydroxy dicarboxylate to 2-oxoglutarate plus NH3.", "reasoning": "Lee et al. (2014) showed that 2-amino-4-hydroxy-4-methylpentanedioic acid decomposes in hydrothermal fluids to 2-oxoglutarate plus ammonia. This route is less direct than the pyruvate-glyoxylate branch but important because it shows chemically richer amino-acid-like precursors can collapse onto the same central keto-acid hub."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Pyruvate-glyoxylate iron route with hydroxylamine finish", "description": "Hydrothermal Fe2+ aldol chemistry builds 2-oxoglutarate from pyruvate and glyoxylate, followed by Fe0/Fe2+-driven reductive amination with hydroxylamine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Pyruvate-glyoxylate iron route with FeS-ammonia finish", "description": "The same hydrothermal C3+C2 assembly route reaches 2-oxoglutarate, but glutamate is completed by FeS-catalyzed reductive amination with ammonia.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Oxalomalate surface handoff with hydroxylamine finish", "description": "Surface oxidative decarboxylation supplies 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, which then enters the hydrothermal iron dehydration-reduction sequence before hydroxylamine amination.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Oxalomalate surface handoff with FeS-ammonia finish", "description": "A surface glyoxylate-cycle analogue generates 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, and hydrothermal processing converts it to glutamate through the FeS-ammonia amination step.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Hydrothermal 2-hydroxyglutarate oxidation plus hydroxylamine amination", "description": "2-Hydroxyglutarate is oxidized in hydrothermal fluids to 2-oxoglutarate and then converted to glutamate by the Fe0/Fe2+-hydroxylamine system.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_008", "rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_005"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Hydrothermal 2-hydroxyglutarate oxidation plus FeS