8)(H,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_002\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"native iron\",\n        \"magnetite\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-380K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"native iron or magnetite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic, H2-rich serpentinization system; pyruvate is one product of multicarbon CO2 reduction\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal-surface CO2 reduction and C-C bond formation generate the C3 alpha-keto acid; the balanced net step is 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvate + 3 H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pyruvate is one of the multicarbon products observed in H2-driven CO2 reduction on hydrothermal Fe-bearing catalysts, as noted in the review's environmental context (Preiner et al., 2020). This is included as an upstream feedstock-extension step rather than a dedicated glutamate experiment, but it is chemically justified in serpentinization settings and connects the network back to simple inorganic carbon.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe2+-catalyzed pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol condensation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"ferrous iron (Fe2+)\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic hydrothermal water; near-neutral to mildly alkaline vent-mixing conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Fe2+-assisted aldol addition of the enolizable pyruvate C3 unit to the glyoxylate aldehyde forms the C5 hydroxyketo diacid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This C3 plus C2 carbon-carbon bond-forming step is directly supported in the iron-driven protometabolic network of Muchowska et al. (2019). Related pyruvate plus glyoxylate coupling also appears in the linked glyoxylate chemistry of Springsteen et al. (2018), reinforcing the prebiotic plausibility of this entry to the 2-oxoglutarate family.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol condensation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-300K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"ambient aqueous linked-glyoxylate-cycle conditions\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Non-enzymatic aldol coupling of pyruvate and glyoxylate in surface water furnishes the same C5 hydroxyketo diacid obtained in hydrothermal iron chemistry.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Springsteen et al. (2018) showed that pyruvate and glyoxylate can furnish 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate at ambient temperature within linked glyoxylate chemistry. This creates a surface entry to the glutamate network, although coupling this ambient step to the hotter downstream iron-mediated chemistry remains an open systems-level question.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"3-oxalomalate decarboxylation to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-300K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"ambient surface water; decarboxylation on hour timescales\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Oxidative decarboxylation of the oxalyl-bearing C6 tricarboxylate liberates CO2 and yields the C5 hydroxyketo diacid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Springsteen et al. (2018) demonstrated decarboxylation of 3-oxalomalate to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate at about 296 K on hour timescales. It is therefore a credible surface source of the immediate C5 precursor feeding the downstream hydrothermal branch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Dehydration of 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"ferrous iron (Fe2+)\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic hydrothermal fluid; can act on surface-delivered mol_008 after transport\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Fe2+-assisted beta-elimination removes water from the C4 hydroxy group to generate the conjugated unsaturated dicarbonyl.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska et al. (2019) reported Fe2+-promoted dehydration of 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate to the unsaturated C5 dicarbonyl. This step is directly supported under hydrothermal iron chemistry; when mol_008 originates at the surface, the required transfer into a hotter iron-rich setting remains a plausible but not yet one-pot-demonstrated systems connection (Muchowska et al., 2019; Springsteen et al., 2018).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Reduction of 4-oxopent-2-enedioate to 2-oxoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"ferrous iron (Fe2+)\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"mildly reducing hydrothermal water with dissolved H2\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reduction of the C=C bond saturates the unsaturated C5 intermediate and yields the alpha-keto diacid 2-oxoglutarate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska et al. (2019) observed iron-promoted reduction of the unsaturated intermediate to 2-oxoglutarate. H2 is written explicitly as the net reductant so the step is stoichiometrically closed; experimentally the reducing power is likely mediated by Fe chemistry and hydrothermal redox gradients rather than free homogeneous H2 alone.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal oxidation of 2-hydroxyglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_011\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-470K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic hydrothermal fluid; pH and redox state control equilibrium\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Dehydrogenation of the alpha-hydroxy acid to the corresponding alpha-keto acid releases H2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Lee et al. (2014) identified 2-hydroxyglutarate to 2-oxoglutarate conversion in hydrothermal fluids and specifically reported oxidation to the keto acid with H2 release. This makes 2-hydroxyglutarate a short entry to the hub molecule, while also highlighting that prolonged hydrothermal exposure can compete with alpha-keto-acid preservation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-promoted reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate with hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_005\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"elemental iron (Fe0)\",\n        \"ferrous iron (Fe2+)\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe0/Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic water; hydroxylamine available; H2 written as net reducing equivalent\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Condensation of the alpha-keto group with hydroxylamine gives an oxime/imino intermediate that is reduced on iron to the alpha-amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska et al. (2019) demonstrated iron-promoted conversion of 2-oxoglutarate to glutamate using hydroxylamine as the nitrogen donor. The graph writes H2 explicitly as the net reducing equivalent so the reaction is redox-balanced; experimentally the reducing power is supplied by Fe0/Fe2+ under hydrothermal conditions. Hydroxylamine availability remains a prebiotic uncertainty, so this route is experimentally supported but geochemically less secure than direct NH3 amination. The product is shown as non-stereospecific glutamate because homochirality is unresolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"FeS-catalyzed reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate with ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_003\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"ferrous sulfide (FeS)\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-400K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"alkaline vent-like, H2-rich conditions; pH 9-11 plausible\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms an imine with the alpha-keto group and FeS facilitates surface-bound reduction to the alpha-amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kitadai et al. (2019) showed FeS promotes reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate with ammonia in alkaline hydrothermal systems. H2 is written explicitly as the net reductant, consistent with H2-rich serpentinization settings; the study establishes the FeS-surface chemistry even though direct efficiency comparisons with the Fe0/Fe2+/hydroxylamine system remain open.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Amination of 2-hydroxypentanedinitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"cyanosulfidic evaporitic pool; NH3-rich; upstream UV chemistry generates the hydroxydinitrile\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amination of the cyanohydrin-derived center converts the hydroxydinitrile to the aminodinitrile with net water elimination.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) reported this NH3-driven conversion within the cyanosulfidic framework. Writing H2O as coproduct closes the net stoichiometry for the amination of the hydroxydinitrile and makes the surface nitrile branch chemically explicit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of 2-aminopentanedinitrile to glutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_015\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"surface aqueous hydrolysis aided by concentration and pH cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential hydration of both nitriles to amides and then carboxylic acids converts the aminodinitrile into the dicarboxylic amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) showed that aminodinitrile hydrolysis yields glutamate. The balanced net transformation requires four waters and releases two equivalents of ammonia; writing NH3 explicitly this way corrects the common under-specified prose shorthand. The long-term stability of the C5 dinitriles under fluctuating UV, pH, and drying remains an open issue noted for cyanosulfidic surface chemistry.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal Fe2+ aldol route with FeS finish\",\n      \"description\": \"Pyruvate and glyoxylate couple under hydrothermal Fe2+, then dehydrate and reduce to 2-oxoglutarate, which is aminated with NH3 on FeS.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal Fe2+ aldol route with hydroxylamine finish\",\n      \"description\": \"The same hydrothermal carbon-skeleton-building sequence ends with Fe0/Fe2+-mediated reductive amination using hydroxylamine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface aldol entry with FeS finish\",\n      \"description\": \"Surface pyruvate plus glyoxylate aldol chemistry generates 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, then a hydrothermal handoff enables dehydration, reduction, and FeS/NH3 amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface aldol entry with hydroxylamine finish\",\n      \"description\": \"A surface aldol step furnishes 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, which then enters hydrothermal dehydration and reduction before Fe0/Fe2+-driven hydroxylamine amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxalomalate entry with FeS finish\",\n      \"description\": \"3-Oxalomalate decarboxylates at the surface to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, which then enters the hydrothermal dehydration, reduction, and FeS amination sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Oxalomalate entry with hydroxylamine finish\",\n      \"description\": \"Surface oxalomalate decarboxylation supplies 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, followed by hydrothermal dehydration, reduction, and Fe0/Fe2+-assisted hydroxylamine amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_004\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"2-Hydroxyglutarate oxidation with FeS finish\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal oxidation of 2-hydroxyglutarate gives 2-oxoglutarate and H2, followed by FeS-catalyzed ammonia incorporation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"2-Hydroxyglutarate oxidation with hydroxylamine finish\",\n      \"description\": \"2-Hydroxyglutarate is oxidized to the keto-acid hub, then Fe0/Fe2+ and hydroxylamine deliver glutamate; additional H2 can be supplied by the vent fluid.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_007\",\n        \"rxn_008\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic dinitrile route\",\n      \"description\": \"A surface nitrile pathway converts 2-hydroxypentanedinitrile to the aminodinitrile and hydrolyzes both nitriles to the dicarboxylic amino acid, bypassing 2-oxoglutarate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_010\",\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_014\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"CO2-fed pyruvate extension of the hydrothermal route\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation first supplies pyruvate; pyruvate then couples with glyoxylate and proceeds through dehydration, reduction, and FeS-catalyzed amination to glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_005\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_006\",\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_010\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_002\",\n    \"mol_003\",\n    \"mol_004\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_008\",\n    \"mol_010\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_009\",\n        \"rxn_010\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_002\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_005\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_008\",\n        \"rxn_009\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glutamate", "overall_strategy": "Represent glutamate as non-stereospecific 2-aminopentanedioic acid. The network is organized around 2-oxoglutarate as the main convergence hub, with four literature-supported entries: hydrothermal Fe2+-mediated pyruvate plus glyoxylate aldol chemistry, surface pyruvate plus glyoxylate aldol chemistry, surface 3-oxalomalate decarboxylation to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, and hydrothermal oxidation of 2-hydroxyglutarate. Ten pathways are obtained by combining these entries with two hydrothermal N-installation reactions and by adding an orthogonal surface dinitrile route that bypasses 2-oxoglutarate. H2, H2O, CO2, and NH3 are written explicitly so redox and hydrolysis steps are stoichiometrically closed and cross-link the branches.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H3NO", "iupac_name": "hydroxylamine", "common_name": "hydroxylamine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3NO/c1-2/h2H,1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H6O6", "iupac_name": "4-hydroxy-2-oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H6O6/c6-2(4(8)9)1-3(7)5(10)11/h2,6H,1H2,(H,8,9)(H,10,11)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C5H4O5", "iupac_name": "4-oxopent-2-enedioic acid", "common_name": null, "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H4O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C5H6O5", "iupac_name": "2-oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "2-oxoglutarate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C5H8O5", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypentanedioic acid", "common_name": "2-hydroxyglutaric acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H8O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3,6H,1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C6H6O8", "iupac_name": "3-oxalomalic acid", "common_name": "3-oxalomalic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C6H6O8/c7-2(5(11)12)1(4(9)10)3(8)6(13)14/h1-2,7H,(H,9,10)(H,11,12)(H,13,14)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C5H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypentanedinitrile", "common_name": null, "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H6N2O/c6-3-1-2-5(8)4-7/h5,8H,1-2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H7N3", "iupac_name": "2-aminopentanedinitrile", "common_name": null, "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H7N3/c6-3-1-2-5(8)4-7/h5H,1-2,8H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H9NO4", "iupac_name": "2-aminopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "glutamate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation to pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["native iron", "magnetite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "370-380K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "native iron or magnetite", "aqueous": "true", "other": "anoxic, H2-rich serpentinization system; pyruvate is one product of multicarbon CO2 reduction"}, "mechanism": "Metal-surface CO2 reduction and C-C bond formation generate the C3 alpha-keto acid; the balanced net step is 3 CO2 + 5 H2 -> pyruvate + 3 H2O.", "reasoning": "Pyruvate is one of the multicarbon products observed in H2-driven CO2 reduction on hydrothermal Fe-bearing catalysts, as noted in the review's environmental context (Preiner et al., 2020). This is included as an upstream feedstock-extension step rather than a dedicated glutamate experiment, but it is chemically justified in serpentinization settings and connects the network back to simple inorganic carbon."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Fe2+-catalyzed pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol condensation", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-350K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Fe2+", "aqueous": "true", "other": "anoxic hydrothermal water; near-neutral to mildly alkaline vent-mixing conditions"}, "mechanism": "Fe2+-assisted aldol addition of the enolizable pyruvate C3 unit to the glyoxylate aldehyde forms the C5 hydroxyketo diacid.", "reasoning": "This C3 plus C2 carbon-carbon bond-forming step is directly supported in the iron-driven protometabolic network of Muchowska et al. (2019). Related pyruvate plus glyoxylate coupling also appears in the linked glyoxylate chemistry of Springsteen et al. (2018), reinforcing the prebiotic plausibility of this entry to the 2-oxoglutarate family."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Surface pyruvate-glyoxylate aldol condensation", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "295-300K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "ambient aqueous linked-glyoxylate-cycle conditions"}, "mechanism": "Non-enzymatic aldol coupling of pyruvate and glyoxylate in surface water furnishes the same C5 hydroxyketo diacid obtained in hydrothermal iron chemistry.", "reasoning": "Springsteen et al. (2018) showed that pyruvate and glyoxylate can furnish 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate at ambient temperature within linked glyoxylate chemistry. This creates a surface entry to the glutamate network, although coupling this ambient step to the hotter downstream iron-mediated chemistry remains an open systems-level question."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "3-oxalomalate decarboxylation to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "295-300K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "ambient surface water; decarboxylation on hour timescales"}, "mechanism": "Oxidative decarboxylation of the oxalyl-bearing C6 tricarboxylate liberates CO2 and yields the C5 hydroxyketo diacid.", "reasoning": "Springsteen et al. (2018) demonstrated decarboxylation of 3-oxalomalate to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate at about 296 K on hour timescales. It is therefore a credible surface source of the immediate C5 precursor feeding the downstream hydrothermal branch."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Dehydration of 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-350K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Fe2+", "aqueous": "true", "other": "anoxic hydrothermal fluid; can act on surface-delivered mol_008 after transport"}, "mechanism": "Fe2+-assisted beta-elimination removes water from the C4 hydroxy group to generate the conjugated unsaturated dicarbonyl.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) reported Fe2+-promoted dehydration of 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate to the unsaturated C5 dicarbonyl. This step is directly supported under hydrothermal iron chemistry; when mol_008 originates at the surface, the required transfer into a hotter iron-rich setting remains a plausible but not yet one-pot-demonstrated systems connection (Muchowska et al., 2019; Springsteen et al., 2018)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Reduction of 4-oxopent-2-enedioate to 2-oxoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-350K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Fe2+", "aqueous": "true", "other": "mildly reducing hydrothermal water with dissolved H2"}, "mechanism": "Reduction of the C=C bond saturates the unsaturated C5 intermediate and yields the alpha-keto diacid 2-oxoglutarate.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) observed iron-promoted reduction of the unsaturated intermediate to 2-oxoglutarate. H2 is written explicitly as the net reductant so the step is stoichiometrically closed; experimentally the reducing power is likely mediated by Fe chemistry and hydrothermal redox gradients rather than free homogeneous H2 alone."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrothermal oxidation of 2-hydroxyglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "370-470K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "anoxic hydrothermal fluid; pH and redox state control equilibrium"}, "mechanism": "Dehydrogenation of the alpha-hydroxy acid to the corresponding alpha-keto acid releases H2.", "reasoning": "Lee et al. (2014) identified 2-hydroxyglutarate to 2-oxoglutarate conversion in hydrothermal fluids and specifically reported oxidation to the keto acid with H2 release. This makes 2-hydroxyglutarate a short entry to the hub molecule, while also highlighting that prolonged hydrothermal exposure can compete with alpha-keto-acid preservation."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Iron-promoted reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate with hydroxylamine", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["elemental iron (Fe0)", "ferrous iron (Fe2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-350K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "Fe0/Fe2+", "aqueous": "true", "other": "anoxic water; hydroxylamine available; H2 written as net reducing equivalent"}, "mechanism": "Condensation of the alpha-keto group with hydroxylamine gives an oxime/imino intermediate that is reduced on iron to the alpha-amino acid.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) demonstrated iron-promoted conversion of 2-oxoglutarate to glutamate using hydroxylamine as the nitrogen donor. The graph writes H2 explicitly as the net reducing equivalent so the reaction is redox-balanced; experimentally the reducing power is supplied by Fe0/Fe2+ under hydrothermal conditions. Hydroxylamine availability remains a prebiotic uncertainty, so this route is experimentally supported but geochemically less secure than direct NH3 amination. The product is shown as non-stereospecific glutamate because homochirality is unresolved."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "FeS-catalyzed reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate with ammonia", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["ferrous sulfide (FeS)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-400K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "FeS", "aqueous": "true", "other": "alkaline vent-like, H2-rich conditions; pH 9-11 plausible"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia forms an imine with the alpha-keto group and FeS facilitates surface-bound reduction to the alpha-amino acid.", "reasoning": "Kitadai et al. (2019) showed FeS promotes reductive amination of 2-oxoglutarate with ammonia in alkaline hydrothermal systems. H2 is written explicitly as the net reductant, consistent with H2-rich serpentinization settings; the study establishes the FeS-surface chemistry even though direct efficiency comparisons with the Fe0/Fe2+/hydroxylamine system remain open."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Amination of 2-hydroxypentanedinitrile", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "295-310K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "cyanosulfidic evaporitic pool; NH3-rich; upstream UV chemistry generates the hydroxydinitrile"}, "mechanism": "Amination of the cyanohydrin-derived center converts the hydroxydinitrile to the aminodinitrile with net water elimination.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) reported this NH3-driven conversion within the cyanosulfidic framework. Writing H2O as coproduct closes the net stoichiometry for the amination of the hydroxydinitrile and makes the surface nitrile branch chemically explicit."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Hydrolysis of 2-aminopentanedinitrile to glutamate", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "295-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "surface aqueous hydrolysis aided by concentration and pH cycling"}, "mechanism": "Sequential hydration of both nitriles to amides and then carboxylic acids converts the aminodinitrile into the dicarboxylic amino acid.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) showed that aminodinitrile hydrolysis yields glutamate. The balanced net transformation requires four waters and releases two equivalents of ammonia; writing NH3 explicitly this way corrects the common under-specified prose shorthand. The long-term stability of the C5 dinitriles under fluctuating UV, pH, and drying remains an open issue noted for cyanosulfidic surface chemistry."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Hydrothermal Fe2+ aldol route with FeS finish", "description": "Pyruvate and glyoxylate couple under hydrothermal Fe2+, then dehydrate and reduce to 2-oxoglutarate, which is aminated with NH3 on FeS.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Hydrothermal Fe2+ aldol route with hydroxylamine finish", "description": "The same hydrothermal carbon-skeleton-building sequence ends with Fe0/Fe2+-mediated reductive amination using hydroxylamine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Surface aldol entry with FeS finish", "description": "Surface pyruvate plus glyoxylate aldol chemistry generates 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, then a hydrothermal handoff enables dehydration, reduction, and FeS/NH3 amination.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Surface aldol entry with hydroxylamine finish", "description": "A surface aldol step furnishes 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, which then enters hydrothermal dehydration and reduction before Fe0/Fe2+-driven hydroxylamine amination.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_003", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Oxalomalate entry with FeS finish", "description": "3-Oxalomalate decarboxylates at the surface to 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, which then enters the hydrothermal dehydration, reduction, and FeS amination sequence.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Oxalomalate entry with hydroxylamine finish", "description": "Surface oxalomalate decarboxylation supplies 4-hydroxy-2-oxoglutarate, followed by hydrothermal dehydration, reduction, and Fe0/Fe2+-assisted hydroxylamine amination.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_004", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "2-Hydroxyglutarate oxidation with FeS finish", "description": "Hydrothermal oxidation of 2-hydroxyglutarate gives 2-oxoglutarate and H2, followed by FeS-catalyzed ammonia incorporation.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "2-Hydroxyglutarate oxidation with hydroxylamine finish", "description": "2-Hydroxyglutarate is oxidized to the keto-acid hub, then Fe0/Fe2+ and hydroxylamine deliver glutamate; additional H2 can be supplied by the vent fluid.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_007", "rxn_008"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_010"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Cyanosulfidic dinitrile route", "description": "A surface nitrile pathway converts 2-hydroxypentanedinitrile to the aminodinitrile and hydrolyzes both nitriles to the dicarboxylic amino acid, bypassing 2-oxoglutarate.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_010", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_014"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "CO2-fed pyruvate extension of the hydrothermal route", "description": "Hydrothermal CO2/H2 fixation first supplies pyruvate; pyruvate then couples with glyoxylate and proceeds through dehydration, reduction, and FeS-catalyzed amination to glutamate.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_005", "rxn_006", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_009", "mol_010"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_004", "mol_006", "mol_008", "mol_010"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_002", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_001"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_003", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_007"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_004", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_011"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_009", "rxn_010"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_006", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_008", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_010", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_007"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_00